#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Every copy of the `pre-commit` task list agrees with `mise.toml`.

MISE description="Every copy of the pre-commit task list agrees with mise.toml"
"""
# `mise.toml` defines `pre-commit` once, and THREE other files restate it. Two of
# them were wrong when this task was written, and the third had been wrong twice:
#
#   * `.githooks/pre-commit` INVOKES the tasks itself when `mise` is absent, so a
#     missing line there is a commit gated by fewer checks than the hook claims.
#     It has been one task short twice.
#   * `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `scripts/install-hooks.sh` DESCRIBE the list. Both said
#     "fmt, clippy on the library" while the list ran five tasks, so `framing`,
#     `anchors` and `anchors-selftest` were invisible in both places a contributor
#     looks first.
#
# `.githooks/pre-commit` predicted the recurrence in its own comment — "THIS LIST
# IS HAND-MAINTAINED AND WILL DRIFT AGAIN ... nothing enforces that" — and that
# sentence is the reason this file exists rather than a fourth repair of the
# strings. A prediction of drift with no check behind it is a note, not a guard.
#
# ── WHY THIS IS A TASK AND NOT A ROW IN `mise run hook-guard` ─────────────────
#
# `hook-guard` is the harness for hook BEHAVIOUR, and it is deliberately in none
# of `gate`, `pre-commit` or `pre-push`, because it commits and pushes. A drift
# check that lived there would run only when somebody remembered to run it — and
# "nothing enforces that" is the exact defect being closed. This check reads text
# and executes nothing, so it has no recursion hazard and can sit in a list.
#
# ── AND IT IS IN `gate` ONLY, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF `anchors-selftest` ──────
#
# It was in `pre-commit` too, following that precedent, and the placement was
# WRONG for a reason specific to this check's subject. `.githooks/pre-commit`
# reaches `mise run pre-commit` only when the staged diff touches `.rs`,
# `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` or `rustfmt.toml`. The drift this task detects is
# introduced by editing `mise.toml`, `.githooks/pre-commit`, `CONTRIBUTING.md` or
# `scripts/install-hooks.sh` — **not one of which matches that filter.** So in
# `pre-commit` it fired only when a Rust file happened to be staged in the same
# commit: a coincidence with respect to its own defect, which is the definition of
# a guard on paper.
#
# `anchors` and `anchors-selftest` earn that placement and this does not, and the
# difference is principled rather than a preference: their subject is break records
# inside `.rs` files, so a commit staging Rust is exactly when their subject
# changes. The filter and their subject agree. Here they do not.
#
# `gate` runs on every push through `pre-push`, so that is where this is certain,
# and one placement that fires beats two where one cannot.
#
# **The residue, stated because it is larger than this task and not this task's to
# fix:** THREE of the five tasks in `pre-commit` — `framing`, `anchors`,
# `anchors-selftest` — check prose and registry content rather than Rust, while the
# filter deciding whether to run them tests for Rust. A commit touching only
# documentation runs none of them. Every commit on the branch that added this file
# was in exactly that position. Widening the filter would make every documentation
# commit run clippy, which contradicts what this hook's header promises, so it is a
# decision rather than a fix and it is left named here.
#
# ── WHAT IT CHECKS, AND THE TAUTOLOGY IT AVOIDS ───────────────────────────────
#
# The obvious version searches each file for each task NAME, and it is worthless
# for the two prose copies: `CONTRIBUTING.md` mentions `framing`, `anchors` and
# `fmt-check` in several unrelated paragraphs, so every name is present whatever
# the copy says. That is `anchors`' own record-quote defect one file over — a
# check satisfied by text that is not the text under test.
#
# So each copy is ANCHORED to the one region that is the copy, and each anchor is
# required to occur EXACTLY ONCE. An anchor that moves gives count 0 and fails
# loudly, which is the direction to be wrong in.
#
# Both directions, for all three copies: every task in the list must appear, AND
# nothing may appear that is not in the list. One direction alone leaves a stale
# leftover invisible — measured twice, once per copy shape, and both are recorded
# in the breaks below.
#
# **How each shape gets its second direction differs, and the difference is where a
# hole hid.** Copy 1 compares WHOLE LINES against the expected invocations, so an
# extra line is extra in both directions by construction. Copies 2 and 3 CAPTURE the
# one cell that is the list and compare it for equality — because the earlier
# spelling searched for the list inside the matched line, and a substring search is
# blind to a name added at either END of what it finds. T9 records that measurement
# at all three positions.
#
# ── AND WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `gate`, which is a different and longer list with its own copies in `README.md`.
# Nothing here says a word about those. This closes the `pre-commit` list because
# that is the list with three copies and a measured history of drifting.
#
# It also never RUNS the fallback. It compares text; whether the hook then behaves
# is `mise run hook-guard`'s question.
#
# ── MADE RED, EVERY BREAK RUN AND WATCHED, EACH NAMING THE ROWS IT REDDENS ─────
#
# **None of these rows is registered in `.mise/tasks/anchors`, deliberately.** That
# registry's record-quote check reads `///`, `//!` and `//` markers and has no `#`
# arm, so it finds zero backticked spans in a shell file and would fail a row here
# for not being quoted by a record that quotes it. Its population walk globs `*.rs`
# only. `scripts/hook-guard.sh`'s header carries the measurement and the exposure
# that follows from it.
#
# Perturbed on a COPY: the four files this task reads, plus `.mise/tasks/`, copied
# into a `git init`-ed temp directory. The task resolves its root from the cwd
# rather than from its own path, so the copy reads the copy, and the tree is never
# written to.
#
# **A TRAP FOR WHOEVER REPLAYS THESE, MEASURED THE HARD WAY.** `python3` on this
# developer's PATH is a mise SHIM, and a shim loads mise config from the cwd — so
# an UNTRUSTED `mise.toml` in the temp directory makes the interpreter exit 1
# before this file runs a single line. Five breaks were recorded as caught before
# the control run was tried and came back red too.
#
# **`scripts/break-hook-record.sh` DOES all of that now, and it exists because the
# sentence that used to close this paragraph was an instruction with nothing behind
# it.** "Always run the unperturbed control first" was advice a replayer had to
# remember, in a file recording what happens when they do not. The script copies the
# tree, runs both checkers as a CONTROL, and refuses to report anything from a
# perturbed run until that control is green:
#
#     scripts/break-hook-record.sh CONTRIBUTING.md from-literal.txt to-literal.txt
#
# It also fails a break that reddens NOTHING, which is the other direction a replay
# lies in, and it aborts when the search literal is not unique. Its own first run
# died on the mise-shim trap above — naming only the copy in
# `MISE_TRUSTED_CONFIG_PATHS` untrusts the real repository — so it names both.
#
# **What it does NOT do is compare the printed rows against the rows a record
# claims.** These records are prose and nothing parses them. So the row lists below
# are still checked by a reader; only the control is mechanical. An unattended green
# from that script means the break reddens something, never that this record is
# honest.
#
# T1. **Drop an invocation.** In `.githooks/pre-commit`, delete the
#     `.mise/tasks/framing` line. Watched:
#
#         FAIL the mise-absent fallback runs `framing`
#
#     **`framing` and not this task, and the swap is worth recording.** While
#     `task-lists` was in the `pre-commit` list, this break deleted its invocation —
#     and the literal could not be a single line, because the paragraph above naming
#     the file that closes the drift had put a second copy of `.mise/tasks/task-lists`
#     into the same hook as a backticked span. `break-once.sh` aborted with `the
#     search literal occurs 2 times`: the same-file trap arriving through the comment
#     documenting the fix for it. Moving the task to `gate` alone removed that
#     invocation, so the break moved to a line whose path this file does not quote —
#     `.mise/tasks/framing` counts 1, measured with `grep -cF` after this record was
#     written. `.mise/tasks/anchors` counts 2 and would abort, which is T6's subject.
#
# T2. **Swap two invocations.** Exchange the `.mise/tasks/anchors` and
#     `.mise/tasks/anchors-selftest` lines. Watched:
#
#         FAIL the fallback runs the tasks in mise.toml's order
#
# T3. **Leave a command behind.** Add `cargo test --doc` under
#     `cargo fmt --all -- --check`. Watched:
#
#         FAIL the fallback runs NOTHING that is not in the list
#
# T4. **Drift an inlined command.** Change `cargo fmt --all -- --check` to
#     `cargo fmt --all`. Watched — TWO rows, because a drifted line is both a
#     missing invocation and an unexplained one, and the pair says so:
#
#         FAIL the mise-absent fallback runs `fmt-check`
#         FAIL the fallback runs NOTHING that is not in the list
#
# T5. **Move the anchor.** Replace the `note: 'mise' is not installed` line with
#     any other text. Watched:
#
#         FAIL the fallback block in .githooks/pre-commit is findable
#
# T6. **THE PREFIX COLLISION, and this break is why the checker was rewritten.**
#     Delete ONLY the `.mise/tasks/anchors` line, leaving
#     `.mise/tasks/anchors-selftest`. Watched:
#
#         FAIL the mise-absent fallback runs `anchors`
#
#     Under the first version of this file it printed `ok:` instead. `find` matched
#     the selftest line, so a MISSING task read as present — the one direction this
#     task exists to catch, in the task built to catch it. T2 was green for the same
#     reason. Both went red once the comparison moved to whole lines.
#
# T7. **Drop a name from the prose.** Remove `framing, ` from `CONTRIBUTING.md`'s
#     `mise run pre-commit` row. Watched:
#
#         FAIL CONTRIBUTING.md names the list exactly
#
# T8. **Drop a name from the installer.** Remove `anchors-selftest, ` from
#     `scripts/install-hooks.sh`'s `pre-commit` line. Watched:
#
#         FAIL scripts/install-hooks.sh names the list exactly
#
# T9. **ADD a name that no longer runs**, which is the direction T7 and T8 cannot
#     see. Put `task-lists, ` into `CONTRIBUTING.md`'s row — and **WHERE in the row
#     is part of the break**, which is exactly what this record used to leave out.
#     Measured at all three positions, control green first, and the same three
#     against `scripts/install-hooks.sh`'s line:
#
#         added at the FRONT     FAIL CONTRIBUTING.md names the list exactly
#         added in the MIDDLE    FAIL CONTRIBUTING.md names the list exactly
#         added at the END       FAIL CONTRIBUTING.md names the list exactly
#
#     **This record was GREEN at both ends until the comparison below was fixed, and
#     it did not say so.** While the check looked for the comma-joined list INSIDE
#     the matched line, a name added at either end left that string intact and
#     satisfied it: FRONT exit 0, MIDDLE exit 1, END exit 0, for both prose copies
#     alike. Because the record named no position, replaying it at the middle
#     confirmed a guard that held in one case out of three — and the failure message
#     printed to the contributor claimed all three.
#
#     The original reason for the break stands unchanged. Taking `task-lists` out of
#     the `pre-commit` list left both prose copies still naming it, and a per-name
#     test was green in both, because every remaining task was still present. A prose
#     copy advertising a check that does not run is the same false claim as one
#     omitting a check that does. The fallback copy already had its reverse
#     direction, so the hole was in the two copies a contributor reads first.
#
# T10. **Reorder the row.** Swap `fmt-check` and `lint-fast` in either prose copy.
#      Watched, one row per copy:
#
#          FAIL CONTRIBUTING.md names the list exactly
#          FAIL scripts/install-hooks.sh names the list exactly
#
#      Recorded because the comparison CLAIMS to pin the order, and until this was
#      run nothing had made that claim fail.
#
# T11. **Move a prose anchor.** Change `| `mise run pre-commit` |` to
#      `| `mise run precommit` |`, or the installer's `echo "  pre-commit  ` to
#      `echo "  precommit  `. Watched, one row per copy, and it is a DIFFERENT row
#      from T9's — the count goes to 0 and the checker says it is lost rather than
#      quietly comparing nothing:
#
#          FAIL the pre-commit description in CONTRIBUTING.md is findable
#          FAIL the pre-commit description in scripts/install-hooks.sh is findable
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys

ROOT = pathlib.Path(
    subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=True,
    ).stdout.strip()
)

MANIFEST = ROOT / "mise.toml"
manifest = MANIFEST.read_text()
failures: list[str] = []


def fail(what: str, *detail: str) -> None:
    failures.append(what)
    print(f"FAIL {what}", file=sys.stderr)
    for line in detail:
        print(f"       {line}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)


def block(name: str) -> str:
    """The body of `[tasks.<name>]`, up to the next table header."""
    at = manifest.find(f"[tasks.{name}]")
    if at < 0:
        sys.exit(f"task-lists: mise.toml has no [tasks.{name}] - this checker is lost")
    rest = manifest[at + 1 :]
    end = rest.find("\n[")
    return rest if end < 0 else rest[:end]


# ── the source of truth ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Parsed with `re` rather than `tomllib`, because the three scripts in this list
# run standalone when `mise` is absent, and the python3 that is then on PATH is
# whatever the machine ships. `tomllib` arrived in 3.11. `anchors` and
# `anchors-selftest` are stdlib-only for the same reason.
ORDER = re.findall(r'\{\s*task\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"\s*\}', block("pre-commit"))
if not ORDER:
    sys.exit("task-lists: could not read [tasks.pre-commit]'s run list from mise.toml")


def invocation(task: str) -> str:
    """What `.githooks/pre-commit` has to contain in order to run `task` itself.

    A FILE task under `.mise/tasks/` is invoked by path. Anything else is a
    `mise.toml` task, and the fallback has to inline its command — so the command
    is read from the manifest rather than copied here, which is what makes a
    DRIFTED inline command a failure and not just a missing one.
    """
    if (ROOT / ".mise" / "tasks" / task).exists():
        return f".mise/tasks/{task}"
    run = re.search(r'^run = "([^"]+)"$', block(task), re.M)
    if run is None:
        sys.exit(
            f"task-lists: [tasks.{task}]'s `run` is not a single quoted command, so "
            "this checker cannot say what the fallback must contain. Teach it, or "
            "keep that task out of `pre-commit`."
        )
    return run.group(1)


# ── copy 1: the hook INVOKES them ─────────────────────────────────────────────
HOOK = ROOT / ".githooks" / "pre-commit"
hook = HOOK.read_text()
NOTE = "echo \"note: 'mise' is not installed - running the same checks directly\" >&2"
if hook.count(NOTE) != 1:
    fail(
        "the fallback block in .githooks/pre-commit is findable",
        f"looked for exactly one line reading: {NOTE}",
        f"found {hook.count(NOTE)}. The anchor moved, or the fallback is gone.",
    )
else:
    # ── WHOLE LINES, and a substring match here was a FALSE GREEN ──────────────
    #
    # The first version asked `fallback.find(want)`, and `.mise/tasks/anchors` is
    # a PREFIX of `.mise/tasks/anchors-selftest`. So deleting the `anchors` line
    # left `find` matching the selftest line, and the checker reported a task as
    # invoked that the hook no longer ran — a missing task looking present, which
    # is the one direction this file exists to catch. The order probe had the same
    # hole and stayed green on a swap of exactly that pair.
    #
    # Measured, not reasoned: with the substring form, swapping those two lines
    # passed. Comparing whole lines closes both at once, and it is the remedy
    # `.mise/tasks/anchors` writes down for its own rot mode 4.
    fallback = hook.split(NOTE, 1)[1]
    expected = [invocation(task) for task in ORDER]
    ran = [
        line.strip()
        for line in fallback.splitlines()
        if line.strip() and not line.strip().startswith("#")
    ]
    for task, want in zip(ORDER, expected):
        if want not in ran:
            fail(
                f"the mise-absent fallback runs `{task}`",
                f"`.githooks/pre-commit` has no line reading: {want}",
                "mise.toml's `pre-commit` list is the authority. That hook runs the",
                "checks itself when mise is missing, so a line missing here is a",
                "commit gated by less than the hook says it gates.",
            )
    if all(want in ran for want in expected):
        order = [ran.index(want) for want in expected]
        if order != sorted(order):
            fail(
                "the fallback runs the tasks in mise.toml's order",
                "every task is invoked, but not in the order the manifest lists them.",
                "The order decides which failure a contributor sees first.",
            )
    # The other direction. A command left behind after a task is removed from the
    # list runs on every commit while nothing claims it should.
    for line in ran:
        if line not in expected:
            fail(
                "the fallback runs NOTHING that is not in the list",
                f"`.githooks/pre-commit` runs: {line}",
                "which is not any task in mise.toml's `pre-commit` list. Either add",
                "the task to the manifest or drop the line.",
            )

# ── copies 2 and 3: they DESCRIBE the list ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each anchored to its single line, so an unrelated paragraph naming a task
# cannot satisfy the check. The pattern CAPTURES the list, and the capture is the
# whole point — see the comparison below.
DESCRIBED = [
    ("CONTRIBUTING.md", r"^\| `mise run pre-commit` \| ([^|]*)\|"),
    ("scripts/install-hooks.sh", r'^echo "  pre-commit\s+([^"]*)"$'),
]
for relative, pattern in DESCRIBED:
    text = (ROOT / relative).read_text()
    found = list(re.finditer(pattern, text, re.M))
    if len(found) != 1:
        fail(
            f"the pre-commit description in {relative} is findable",
            f"expected exactly one line matching: {pattern}",
            f"found {len(found)}. Re-anchor this row, or restore the line.",
        )
        continue
    # Named distinctly, and not `line` or `expected`. Both of those are already
    # bound by the fallback loop above, and a first version of this block relied on
    # one of them — it read the CLIPPY COMMAND left over from that loop and reported
    # a failure about CONTRIBUTING.md while never looking at the file. It failed
    # closed, which is luck: a leaked value that happened to contain the list would
    # have passed both prose rows without reading either file.
    line = found[0].group(0)
    described = found[0].group(1).strip()
    # ── THE CELL IS CAPTURED AND COMPARED, because SEARCHING INSIDE THE LINE was
    # ── one-directional at both ends of it. This is a measured repair, not a
    # ── tidy-up.
    #
    # The version before this asked `wanted not in described`, where `described` was
    # the WHOLE matched line. A substring test cannot see a name added at either END
    # of the list, because the wanted string survives intact on both sides of it.
    # Measured on a copy at all three positions, control green first, for both prose
    # copies alike:
    #
    #     task-lists added at the FRONT    exit 0  GREEN   <- hole
    #     added in the MIDDLE              exit 1  RED
    #     added at the END                 exit 0  GREEN   <- hole
    #
    # So the reverse direction held only where an inserted name happened to split
    # the comma-joined run. Capturing the cell and comparing it with `!=` closes
    # both ends, and it is genuinely bidirectional: a missing task, an extra task
    # anywhere in the row, and a reordering each change the cell.
    #
    # The earlier version's own argument was sound and its implementation was not,
    # which is worth keeping apart. A name-by-name reverse test really cannot work:
    # task names collide as substrings — `fmt` inside `fmt-check`, `lint` inside
    # `lint-fast`, `anchors` inside `anchors-selftest` — and the surrounding prose
    # names real tasks in passing, CONTRIBUTING.md's own row ending "is not a gate"
    # while `gate` is a task. The comma-joined string is still the right thing to
    # compare. It has to be compared against a CELL rather than looked for in a
    # line.
    #
    # That is what the anchor patterns capture: the third column of the markdown row
    # is prose and must stay out of the comparison, so the group stops at the next
    # `|`, and the installer's group stops at the closing quote.
    wanted = ", ".join(ORDER)
    if described != wanted:
        fail(
            f"{relative} names the list exactly",
            f"expected this row to name exactly: {wanted}",
            f"it names:                          {described}",
            f"the line reads: {line.strip()}",
            "Compared as one string against the whole cell: it fails when a task is",
            "missing, when one is named that no longer runs ANYWHERE in the row, and",
            "when the order drifts. It is read before anybody runs the hook, so both",
            "directions are a claim to a contributor about what does and does not",
            "check their commit.",
        )

if failures:
    print(f"{len(failures)} copy of the pre-commit list disagrees with mise.toml"
          if len(failures) == 1
          else f"{len(failures)} checks failed against mise.toml's pre-commit list",
          file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)
print(f"ok: every copy of the pre-commit list agrees with mise.toml "
      f"({', '.join(ORDER)})")
